WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.590 --> 00:00:11.220
We're witnessing the economic and political
meltdown of Venezuela, one of South America's

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:13.010
most important economies.

00:00:13.010 --> 00:00:17.340
The President of the United States doesn't
want a meltdown in Venezuela on his watch.

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:18.470
And that's understandable.

00:00:18.470 --> 00:00:22.590
Unfortunately, that regime is unsustainable
today.

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:27.779
And a policy that encourages the democratic
opposition to have a dialogue with a repressive

00:00:27.779 --> 00:00:29.940
regime really isn't sustainable.

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:34.820
The United States could be part of a process,
part of a solution to hold the Maduro government

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:39.690
accountable to its own constitution, and to
its commitments to defend democracy.

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:42.780
President Obama has an opportunity to use
diplomatic tools...

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:47.880
Working with key neighbors that are becoming
more active in the defense of democracy.

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:54.149
The President also has some unique, asymmetrical
tools that he can use to sanction individuals

00:00:54.149 --> 00:00:57.680
within the Venezuelan regime who do not want
to see a democratic transition.

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:00.079
It remains to be seen whether President Obama

00:01:00.079 --> 00:01:03.970
will be part of that solution, or whether
he'll preside over the consolidation of a

00:01:03.970 --> 00:01:06.500
dictatorship in South America.

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:10.740
We all want stability in Venezuela, but at the cost of keeping a corrupt

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:13.200
and incompetent government in power? What do you think?

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:15.480
Let us know in the comments, and let us know

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:19.440
what other topics you want AEI scholars to cover in 60 seconds.

